  "query_preview": "/Wednesday through Sunday/... \nModel guidance continues to be consistent in indicating another more \nactive period materializing Wednesday into the first half of \nThursday. The flow by Wednesday is forecast to shift out of the \nsouth as the subtropical high shifts into Texas and a upper-level \nlow meanders off the California coast. This southerly flow is likely \nto bring additional moisture northward from Mexico, with projected \nPWATs increasing to 1.7-2.0\" and low-level mixing ratios increasing \nto 12-13 g/kg. In addition, there will likely more favorable upper-\nlevel forcing from the aforementioned upper-level low in the form of \ndivergence aloft as well as the potential of an easterly wave moving \ninto the region from Mexico as indicated by both the GFS and ECMWF. \nThe more favorable upper-level forcing is likely to enhance the \nconvective activity, with flash flooding becoming more of a threat \ngiven the deeper moisture profiles that will be in place. The latest \nNBM PoPs for Wednesday through the first half of Thursday remain \nelevated, ranging between 40-60% across much of south-central and \nsoutheast AZ. \n\nHeading towards the end of the week and next weekend, the guidance \nis in good agreement in showing the subtropical high relocating \ntowards the Gulf Coast Region while the aforementioned upper-\nlevel low remains situated off the west coast. This pattern \nconfiguration will cause the flow to shift more out of the \nsouthwest, bringing in a drier airmass as PWATs drop under \n1.2-1.3\". As a result, an overall downtrend in thunderstorm \nactivity is expected, with afternoon activity limited to the \neastern third of AZ. \n\nTemperatures during the middle to latter half of the week will run \nslightly below normal as afternoon highs will top out in the low \n100s across the south-central AZ lower deserts to the mid 100s \nacross the western deserts. In fact, with the potential for abundant \ncloud cover as well as lingering rainfall activity, afternoon highs \non Thursday may struggle to reach the triple digits across the south-\ncentral AZ lower deserts. Temperatures will gradually rebound Friday \nand into next weekend but remain near seasonal levels with no \nextreme heat in sight for at least the next seven days.",
